>h2 What Is CupidC
>tree open What Is CupidC
CupidC is the in-OS C-like compiler used for most shell programs in /bin.

Source files: kernel/lang/cupidc.cc, kernel/lang/cupidc_lex.cc,
              kernel/lang/cupidc_parse.cc, kernel/lang/cupidc_string.cc,
              kernel/lang/cupidc_elf.cc

CupidC has three modes:
1) JIT run:
   cupidc file.cc
2) AOT to ELF:
   ccc file.cc -o output
3) Compile + disassemble (no run):
   cc -d file.cc

Shell also auto-discovers and runs:
- /bin/<name>.cc
- /home/bin/<name>.cc
So if a file exists there, you can often just type:
  <name>
>endtree

>h2 Entry Point
>tree open Entry Point
Use:
  void main() { ... }
or:
  int main() { ... return 0; }

The HolyC-style top-level statement form does not require a main function.
When both forms appear, top-level statements run before main().
>endtree

>h2 Command-Line Arguments
>tree open Command-Line Arguments
CupidC programs read args via:
  char *args = get_args();

Example:
  void main() {
    char *args = get_args();
    if (args && args[0]) {
      print("args: ");
      println(args);
    } else {
      println("no args");
    }
  }
>endtree

>h2 Hello Example
>tree open Hello Example
  void main() {
    println("Hello from CupidC");
    print("cwd: ");
    println(get_cwd());
  }

Save as /home/bin/hello.cc, then run:
  hello
>endtree

>h2 Language Features
>tree open Language Features
Supported core features include:
- int, char, void, pointers
- sized types: U0, U8, U16, U32, I8, I16, I32, U64, I64, Bool
- C compatibility spellings: long, short, signed, unsigned
- float, double, float4, double2
- structs (including field access)
- class keyword with methods (HolyC-style)
- arrays
- if / else
- ternary conditional operator (cond ? a : b)
- while / for / do-while
- switch / case / default
- break / continue / return
- labels and goto
- function definitions and calls (including forward calls via two-pass)
- new / del (kmalloc+zero-init / kfree+null)
- reg / noreg / extern / inline / register / restrict hints
- typedef and enum support
- comma-separated private typedef declarators; value and pointer aliases keep
  their own pointer depth
- one-dimensional fixed-array typedefs in automatic, global, block-static,
  structure, class, and persistent REPL storage; parameters decay to element
  pointers, while sizeof keeps the complete array size; record-element reads
  and writes may continue after indexing an array member
- anonymous and tagged structure typedefs in private JIT, AOT, and REPL source;
  alias chains and structure-pointer aliases keep the field layout, and a
  failed REPL line cannot fill an older forward tag
- private member addresses through &record.field and &pointer->field; the
  pointer form loads the pointed-to record before adding the field offset
- enum and array bounds can use constant integer expressions
- private fixed allocations check positive counts, byte products, record
  layout, data capacity, and cumulative local frames before reservation
- signed constant integer arithmetic rejects overflow; an unsigned operand
  wraps modulo 2^32
- private unsigned 32-bit objects, pointers, parameters, returns, fields,
  arrays, enums, conditional arms, sizeof results, comparisons, division,
  remainder, and shifts retain unsigned semantics; /= and >>= use the same
  rules, while private %= syntax remains open
- unsigned 32-bit values convert exactly to float or double across the sign
  boundary, including function and method returns; float or double to unsigned
  32-bit remains unsupported
- all 40 uint32_t, size_t, and swap_handle_t kernel results publish the private
  unsigned lane; uint8_t and uint16_t results remain integer-promoted
- the private compiler accepts __attribute__((...)) compatibility spellings
- basic inline asm blocks
- arithmetic and logical operators
- #pragma once

Unary signs:
- Unary plus accepts char, int, float, and double.
- Unary minus uses integer NEG for char and int.
- Float and double negation changes only the IEEE-754 sign bit. Signed zero
  and every other payload bit remain intact.
- Other operand types report a focused error. The failed expression is rolled
  back, so the next REPL expression can still compile.

The private compiler tags loops and switches separately. Break exits the
nearest control. Continue finds the nearest loop and removes every crossed
switch selector before jumping to the loop target. While continues at its
condition, do-while at its condition trampoline, and for at its iteration.
The parser accepts 128 active loop-or-switch controls and 1,024 active
statement calls. It rejects the next entry before further recursion and
restores both counters after a failed REPL evaluation.

Current toolchain gaps:
- C and GNU-extension coverage is incomplete.
- Macro preprocessing and unsupported-form diagnostics remain incomplete.
- The in-OS command compiles one translation unit per invocation; separate
  compilation and multi-file linking still need toolchain support.
>endtree

>h2 Shared Bootstrap Type Graph
>tree open Shared Bootstrap Type Graph
The shared CupidC frontend recognizes the native type spellings when it parses
Cupid source:
- U0 is void.
- U8, U16, U32, and U64 are unsigned integers of 1, 2, 4, and 8 bytes.
- I8, I16, I32, and I64 are the matching signed integers.
- Bool and bool use the signed 4-byte int identity.
- float4 and double2 are distinct 16-byte vector types with 16-byte alignment.

Strict C11 mode leaves those names available to ordinary declarations and
typedefs. A malformed mixed type reports the second specifier and leaves the
parser ready for another source in the same job. Under the Cupid profile,
unchanged kernel/cpu/simd_intrin.h publishes all 29 intrinsic bindings.
>endtree

>h2 Normal Build Ownership
>tree open Normal Build Ownership
The checked CupidC seed compiles 238 checked-in sources in the normal image
build. The generated kernel symbol source adds one checked transform, bringing
the normal total to 239 CupidC transforms. All 239 sources use .cc. The
19-source i386 Linux fixed point uses the same names, including its five roots
shared with the normal image. Native GCC or Clang rules select C explicitly
with -x c, while the checked seed compiles those paths under its fixed target
profile.
The complete 20-source crypto cohort includes:
- AES and AES-GCM
- ChaCha20 and ChaCha20-Poly1305
- constant-time helpers, HMAC, and HKDF
- Poly1305, SHA-256, and SHA-512
- bigint, RSA, X25519, P-256, ECDSA, and Ed25519
- ASN.1, X.509 parsing, and X.509 chain handling
- the CSPRNG and its CPU entropy probes

The established driver and kernel group also includes ACPI and MP-table
discovery, e1000, the desktop shell, the socket layer, TCP, ATA, keyboard,
mouse, PCI, PIT, RTC, RTL8139, speaker, VGA, AC'97, system calls, the shell,
EHCI, and UHCI. Seventy-one more unchanged sources cover audio, filesystems,
graphics, GUI, language services, memory, networking, SMP, TLS, USB, and
utility code. The shared ctool.cc, cupidasm.cc, cupiddis.cc, elf32.cc, and x86.cc
implementations complete the established 116-source group. ADRs 0115 and 0123
transferred the first 28 source-driven roots. ADR 0124 renames another 111
roots used only by production CupidC. ADR 0126 completes the fixed-point
naming work, ADR 0129 transfers the lexer, ADR 0135 transfers Nuked OPL3,
ADR 0139 transfers JPEG decoding and glyph rasterization, ADR 0167 transfers
the FPU and SMP roots, ADR 0176 transfers libm, ADR 0180 transfers the kernel
entry and SIMD roots, ADR 0181 transfers the string root, and ADR 0184
transfers all 83 Doom and platform roots. The checked production path owns
kernel/core/kernel.cc, kernel/cpu/simd.cc, kernel/core/string.cc, and every
Doom .cc source.

Each result is checked as an i386 ELF32 relocatable object before it replaces
the build output. A valid data-only object may omit .text, but its other
sections and symbols must still pass validation. The strict frontier must
compile all 155 checked-in roots twice. The full frontier now passes with a
445-input path snapshot whose SHA-256 is
99d03de14f544f6a76d21ed147e62018873f1e2e8dfa2f4459830b69314432c2.
Both 155-object sets are byte-identical; each totals 3,749,796 bytes. The combined
graph passes clean normal and partitioned image builds. Final frontier
publication retries a short permission-style directory lock with five bounded
delays. A persistent lock leaves the frontier unpublished. The input scan
ignores hidden paths under active include roots, so private compiler staging
headers from a concurrent build do not look like repository drift. Strong
four-vCPU runs pass with e1000 and RTL8139 networking. They start all four CPUs, seed
from RDRAND, pass all 62 crypto checks, exercise USB storage and audio, reach
the desktop and terminal, and complete in-OS CupidC execution. Both NIC runs
print [fpu] boot smoke ok and FPU boot smoke passed, then finish
feature16_asm_fpu.cc.

The production path generates kernel/cpu/ksyms_data.cc after the pass-one
link. Its normal recipe uses the checked CupidC wrapper. The source stores the
packed symbol blob as little-endian unsigned int words and records its
meaningful byte length separately. Zero padding completes the last word but
is not part of that length. Generation runs private snapshots of the pass-one
kernel and checked seed. Checked CupidDis emits canonical text, and checked
CupidObj turns that exact text into the source. Python independently renders
the expected bytes and rejects malformed rows, missing output, mismatches, or
live input drift before atomic publication. The compiler wrapper freezes the
source and its two-header closure before object validation and publication.
The pass-one and final kernels project the same text symbols at the same
addresses.

Across root and supplemental builds, the ownership audit reports 245 CupidC
transforms and no host C transform. Host Python participates in all 449
transforms. The normal Toolchain build uses checked static i386 tools for its
contract cohort; native contract binaries are optional oracles. The
publisher accepts only a dedicated cupidc-contracts directory inside the
source tree. It validates the target before work and again before promotion.
An existing destination must already verify as a complete cohort. Arbitrary
directories, source trees, files, and symbolic links remain untouched.
Initial, private, and newly discovered contract inventories must match exactly,
which catches additions, removals, and restored edits that changed a copied
input. Every run derives the cohort from its requested executable, requires a
named manifest artifact, and verifies the manifest, target, fixed-point
record, filenames, sizes, artifact hashes, 45 contract input hashes, the
checked seed manifest, and the 41-file fixed-point source inventory before
execution. The contract inventory now has 45 inputs, including the Toolchain
Makefile and both Python modules that construct or verify the cohort. The
manifest digest, decoded data, schema checks, and build plan
come from one captured byte sequence. The 438-transform normal image has no
host C or recursive Make transform. Its
CupidASM, CupidObj, CupidLD, and CupidDis commands run from the checked seed.

The normal build compiles kernel/gfx/jpeg.cc and
kernel/gfx/glyph_raster.cc through the checked seed. Their closed header sets
produce deterministic 21,120-byte and 11,744-byte objects. A byte-fixed
baseline JPEG in the ISO exercises jpeg_decode_mem inside Cupid OS, checks
its 8-by-8 dimensions and all 64 pixels, and frees the decoded allocation.
A second guest check matches Liberation Mono, requests an unused size-37 Q
glyph twice, and requires a positive cached width. The strong e1000 and
RTL8139 smokes require both success markers.

The checked seed finalizes C11 inline linkage after reading the complete
translation unit. The ordinary header declaration and inline definition of
OPL3_Generate4Ch in kernel/audio/nuked_opl3.cc provide one global function.
Two complete kernel-profile compiles produce the same validated 40,424-byte
object, with only memset undefined. Prior static linkage stays internal, and
an external-linkage inline declaration requires a definition in the same
translation unit. The wrapper compiles from a private copy of the source and
its three headers, then rejects live input drift before publishing the object.
A pure external inline definition remains outside hosted emission. The closed
production recipe, frontier, image builds, and dual-NIC runtime gates pass.

The final boot proof must run production code from the complete cohort. Its 62
checks cover cryptographic primitives, positive and negative ASN.1 inputs,
X.509 parsing and names, chain state, and embedded-root lookup. That last path
uses the external CA array whose missing bound once blocked CupidC. It is a
runtime ownership check, not a claim that certificate trust validation is
complete.

The CSPRNG path represents typed GNU assembly outputs and a matching input,
emits RDTSC, CPUID, RDRAND, and SETC through Cupid's x86 model, and preserves
EBX. The four-vCPU GUI contract must reach SMP, all 62 crypto checks, e1000
traffic, the desktop, terminal, and CupidC execution at 0x01100000. The
e1000 and RTL8139 contracts still cover keyboard and mouse detach and
reattach, ATA, AC'97 and PC speaker audio, EHCI storage across six lifetimes,
a zero-padded RTC timestamp, and DHCP traffic. A separate runtime check must
load the same external ELF program twice at 0x01C00000 and release its first
arena lease before the second load. CupidC optimization, removal of the Host
Python and WSL launch bridge, a native Windows fixed point, IWAD-backed
gameplay, and a defined output-quality gate remain open.

CupidC also represents operand-free GNU assembly inside functions. Basic
statements and extended statements with an empty output list are implicitly
volatile. Exact PAUSE, NOP, STI, HLT, CLI, CLD, SFENCE, and FNINIT sequences
emit without a temporary frame slot or EBX traffic. The checked seed
builds the normal e1000, desktop, socket, and TCP objects with this path.

In the checked seed, an exact empty volatile extended template with one memory
clobber and no operands remains a compiler ordering point and emits no
instruction bytes. This compiles the unchanged Doom sound driver through its
production CupidC recipe.

The checked seed keeps file-scope GNU basic assembly in a separate
translation-unit table. Linear IR preserves each template in source order
without treating it as a function statement. The i386 emitter recognizes the
twelve exact x87/SSE floating wrapper definitions at the start of the
then-named kernel/cpu/libm.c and writes prologue-free global functions through Cupid's
shared x86 encoder. The fixture has 248 text bytes and no relocations. The
checked seed accepts named operands on function-body GNU assembly and
resolves %[name] to the existing numeric operand before IR.

Checked-seed CupidC recognizes both the historical and active dg_setjmp and
dg_longjmp file-scope blocks in kernel/doom/dglibc.cc. Active dg_setjmp is a
31-byte global function that saves ESP plus four; dg_longjmp remains 38 bytes.
Both emit through Cupid's x86 model without relocation. A changed template or
mismatched declaration is rejected.

Checked-seed CupidC recognizes GNU returns_twice on file-scope function
declarations and merges the attribute across compatible redeclarations. A
marked function must remain a direct call target; conversion to a function
pointer is rejected.
Supported calls use four-byte cdecl arguments and may return void or any
nonaggregate type. Before a supported call, the emitter saves the live
four-byte expression operands below the arguments in frame slots owned by that
call. It restores those words after caller cleanup and then publishes the call
result.

A live-prefix call fails if any returns-twice continuation can reach it again.
A marked call with no live prefix may repeat. Aggregate, wide-integer, and
wider-than-four-byte floating arguments and aggregate results fail explicitly.
A decoder-driven i386 oracle models first and second returns with transfer
values zero and seven. The separate guest diagnostic runs the active landing
paths in Cupid OS.

Active dg_setjmp saves ESP plus four, which is the caller stack after a normal
return. Its declaration uses returns_twice. dg_longjmp, dg_exit, and dg_abort
use noreturn. The guest dglibc_test executes direct longjmp and exit landings,
then runs two quit and two error sessions to check callback order, filtering,
and cleanup between Doom launches.

The checked seed emits the complete x87 statements in libm_pow_impl and
libm_powf_impl. The double form accepts one double output and four double
inputs. The mixed form accepts one float output, two float inputs, and two
double inputs. Both use a memory clobber. Linear IR evaluates each set of
five addresses once in source order. Each focused function has 116 exact
text bytes, no relocations, a maximum x87 depth of three, and balanced depth
on return. All seven active range-reduction sites use corrected fsubr
%st, %st(1) or its escaped statement form. CupidC emits FSUB ST(1), ST(0)
as DC E9 for x - round(x). The old fsub spelling still emits DC E1 and
remains covered as a compatibility case.

The checked seed also emits the exact volatile sqrtsd %1, %0 statement. It
accepts one modifiable, non-atomic double =x output, one non-atomic double x
input, and no clobbers. Linear IR evaluates the output address before the
input value. The 65-byte focused function uses the shared MOVSD and SQRTSD
encodings and has no relocations.

The checked seed also emits the exact volatile x87 statement in
libm_atan2_impl. It accepts one modifiable, non-atomic double =m output, two
addressable, non-atomic double m inputs in y, x order, and one memory
clobber. Linear IR evaluates all three addresses once in source order. The
53-byte focused function uses the shared model for both loads, FPATAN, and
the final store, with no relocations.

The checked seed also emits the exact volatile x87 statement in libm_exp_impl.
It accepts one modifiable, non-atomic double =m output, two addressable,
non-atomic double m inputs in x, log2e order, and one memory clobber. Linear
IR evaluates all three addresses once in source order. The 71-byte focused
function has no relocations, reaches x87 depth three, and returns to its
incoming depth.

The checked seed also emits the exact aligned fabs mask block and the following
fabs and fabsf wrappers. The masks occupy the first 32 bytes of .rodata, with
local NOTYPE labels at offsets 0 and 16. The wrappers contain 15 and 14 text
bytes and carry one R_386_32 relocation each to the matching label.

The checked seed also emits the following floor, floorf, ceil, ceilf, round,
roundf, trunc, and truncf wrappers. Each saves the incoming x87 control word,
selects the source rounding mode, runs FRNDINT, restores the original word,
and returns through XMM0. The four pairs select down, up, nearest-even, and
toward-zero mode. They add 384 text bytes without relocations, reach x87
depth one, and balance ESP and x87 depth.

The checked seed also emits the exact fmod and fmodf wrappers. Each loads y
below x, repeats FPREM while status-word C2 remains set, and uses a checked
short backward branch. It discards ST(1), returns the remainder through XMM0
at the source width, and restores ESP and x87 depth. Both wrappers contain 35
text bytes and no relocation.

The checked seed also emits the aligned libm_log2e_const and libm_ln2_const
block and the following exp2, exp2f, exp, expf, log2, log2f, log, and logf
wrappers. The constants occupy 16 read-only bytes at alignment eight. The
functions add 264 text bytes. The natural forms carry four R_386_32
relocations in total, while the base-two forms need none. All eight balance
ESP and x87 depth and reach no deeper than three x87 values. The full source
then reaches pow on line 846.

The checked seed emits pow, powf, asin, asinf, acos, acosf, sinh, sinhf,
cosh, coshf, tanh, tanhf, cbrt, cbrtf, hypot, hypotf, nextafter, and
nextafterf. These wrappers copy their original cdecl argument words, call the
matching external libm implementation, reclaim the copies, and move the
ST(0) result into XMM0. Four shared unary or binary float or double shapes
add 558 text bytes and 18 R_386_PC32 relocations with addend -4. Two complete
kernel-profile compiles of corrected kernel/cpu/libm.cc produce the same
16,164-byte ELF32 relocatable object with SHA-256
c0911732361f2e1ea78aa778f834719ba12208cc2d9f0a312455a5e6a38a75b4.

General GAS input and other file-scope templates remain unsupported. The
normal libm.cc recipe uses the checked CupidC wrapper with a frozen closure of
kernel/core/types.h and kernel/cpu/libm.h. The source remains 43,736 bytes and
has SHA-256
baffe801c7573b8500c60251298a753f60732608d58443178be8ce9ab809ef93.
The guest gate runs /bin/feature15_libm.cc and requires the seven-case x87
summary, all 29 checks, and PASS feature15_libm. ADR 0209 records the
runtime-tested numerical correction.

The checked seed represents the exact per-CPU pointer output
mov %%gs:0, %0 with one four-byte =r object or void pointer. It keeps the
pointer type, evaluates the destination once, and emits the absolute GS load
as 65 A1 00 00 00 00.

The checked seed accepts independent r and c inputs for the privileged
assembly in kernel/cpu/idt.cc, kernel/mm/paging.cc, and kernel/smp/lapic.cc.
A represented four-byte integer or data pointer can use r. A represented
four-byte integer can use c and arrives in ECX. The i386 emitter writes exact
CR0, CR2, CR3, and CR4 moves and RDMSR into ELF32 objects without a host
assembler.

The normal recipes compile these roots to byte-identical validated objects.
Their sizes are 8,756 bytes for idt.cc, 2,336 bytes for paging.cc, and 4,184
bytes for lapic.cc. Exact checked wrappers block host compiler fallback.
WRMSR and unsupported control-register directions remain open, along with
fixed EBX and q inputs, arbitrary templates, and general clobbers.

The checked seed also represents the exact volatile fxsave (%0) form used twice
in kernel/core/process.cc. Its independent r input must be a four-byte object
or void pointer, and the statement retains its memory clobber. Linear IR
evaluates the pointer once. The shared x86 model emits 0F AE 00 at the address
in EAX without a temporary frame slot. Two complete KERNEL_I386 compiles
produce the same validated 30,216-byte object, with FXSAVE at text offsets
0x1967 and 0x4d7c. The normal process.cc recipe uses the checked wrapper.

The checked seed accepts three exact volatile machine-state memory outputs.
fnstsw %0 and fnstcw %0 write a modifiable 16-bit integer through =m.
stmxcsr %0 writes a modifiable 32-bit integer. Linear IR evaluates each
destination once, and the i386 emitter writes through that address with the
shared x86 model. Other =m templates remain unsupported. The separate exact
call-next support also handles the later local-label statement in unchanged
panic.cc. Two full kernel-profile compiles produce the same validated
10,212-byte ELF32 object. The normal panic.cc recipe uses the checked wrapper.

The checked seed handles integer atomic load, store, exchange, fetch-add, and
fetch-or builtins for one-, two-, and four-byte objects. It checks constant
memory orders and emits ordinary loads and release stores, memory XCHG, LOCK
XADD, or a LOCK CMPXCHG retry loop. Fetch-or rebuilds its desired value after
contention, returns the old value, and preserves EBX. The six order names are
target predefines in every language mode, while the expressions remain
GNU-only. A decoded i386 model checks old results, memory changes, narrow
signedness, wraparound, cdecl state, one-time operand evaluation, and a
forced competing update. Runtime orders, pointer atomics, HLE flags, and
eight-byte atomics remain open. The checked seed carries all five operations
and compiles the active EHCI fetch-or path.

The checked seed parses all eight unchanged helpers in
kernel/core/ports.h. The
scalar IN and OUT forms keep their one-, two-, and four-byte accumulator
widths and use the 16-bit DX port. The repeated word forms retain their
read/write buffer and count operands. INSW also retains its memory clobber.
The emitter writes back the final pointer and count and preserves ESI or EDI
across the cdecl call.

The scalar forms emit EC, EE, 66 ED, 66 EF, ED, and EF. INSW and OUTSW emit
FC F3 66 6D and FC F3 66 6F through the shared x86 model. The checked-seed C11
standalone sweep passes 157 of 159 active non-Doom headers. scheduler.h and
simd_intrin.h remain exact C11-profile failures. The checked seed parses all 29
simd_intrin.h declarations under the Cupid profile through the shared native
type graph.

The checked seed carries this port-I/O capability. The normal build uses it in
the 155-source checked-in CupidC cohort. It emits unchanged kernel/smp/acpi.cc
and kernel/smp/mp_tables.cc with the checked seed. Their 5,708-byte and
4,156-byte objects are deterministic and validated. Exact recursive header
closures and poisoned host recipes protect each transferred build rule.

The checked seed also retains the exact Nd port constraint used by the 8259 PIC.
It selects the DX alternative and emits the unchanged 8-bit IN and OUT
templates through the shared x86 model. The complete kernel/cpu/pic.cc source
produces a deterministic ELF32 object under the kernel profile. The normal PIC
recipe uses the checked CupidC wrapper.
>endtree

>h2 Self-Hosting Compiler Path
>tree open Self-Hosting Compiler Path
Cupid OS has a shared CupidC frontend, linear IR, and ELF32 emitter for the
self-hosting migration. This path is separate from the in-OS JIT and AOT
compiler described elsewhere in this guide.

The shared path allocates target-sized i386 stack storage for referenced fixed
arrays and records with alignment up to four bytes. It carries one-byte,
two-byte, and four-byte integers through locals, file objects, members, indexed
access, conditions, conversions, assignment, mutation, and prototyped,
variadic, or unprototyped direct and indirect calls. Narrow loads extend into
canonical 32-bit values. Stores use the declared byte or word
width. Byte and word compound assignments and increment or decrement compute through 32-bit
integer promotion, evaluate their destination once, and store once at the
declared width. Signed byte and word mutation wraps through the low i386 lane
and then sign-extends the result. Represented cdecl scalar arguments keep
four-byte stack slots, and callers and callees normalize narrow results.

The shared path also carries signed and unsigned eight-byte integer values.
Full-width constants, matching conditional arms, fixed direct and indirect
call results, object loads, declared parameters, and named call arguments use
one Linear IR handle backed by a private eight-byte frame snapshot. File
objects, block statics, fixed automatic objects, pointer dereferences,
ordinary members, and indexed elements can be initialized, loaded, assigned,
mutated, chained, discarded, and returned. A declared wide argument occupies
eight cdecl stack bytes, and later parameter addresses include its full width. The
i386 return boundary places the low word in EAX and the high word in EDX.
Addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, remainder, unary plus, unary
minus, bitwise complement, left shift, signed or unsigned right shift, AND,
OR, XOR, all six signed or unsigned comparisons, logical not, short-circuit logical operators,
conditional selection, structured scalar conditions, signed or unsigned
switch dispatch, all ten compound assignments, prefix and postfix update, and
conversion to or from represented byte, word, and doubleword integers use the
same snapshot path. A wide switch evaluates its condition once, duplicates the
snapshot handle, and compares both words of each case value. Mutation evaluates
the destination once and keeps one semantic load and store. Signed long long
converts to unsigned long long when the usual arithmetic rules select the
unsigned type. In GNU mode, a wide enum promotes to its exact compatible signed
or unsigned wide type. The complete
ctool_buffer_put_le64, ctool_buffer_patch_le64, pp_if_value_truth,
pp_if_is_negative, pp_if_signed_less, pp_if_signed_magnitude, and X25519
fe_carry have exact source guards. Focused fixtures lower and emit the required
operation shapes. CupidASM's number parser and unary expression branch guard
the arithmetic, and X25519's fe_mul_u32 helper guards wide-by-narrow
multiplication. Division and remainder use a fixed
64-step restoring loop over unsigned magnitudes, then apply the quotient or
dividend sign. The complete
cfront_constant_apply_binary body guards signed and unsigned quotient and
remainder, while CupidASM's number parser guards an active wide divisor. Cases
that C leaves undefined promise neither a runtime trap nor a result. Signed and
unsigned wide integers can also pass through an ellipsis or a call without a
prototype.

The shared path carries float and double values through objects, assignment,
calls, variadic reads, and returns. Explicit casts and assignment conversion
work in either direction. Unary plus and minus and binary addition,
subtraction, multiplication, and division accept matching or mixed floating
widths. Matching floating conditional arms keep their width. Mixed arithmetic
and conditional arms use double. The four arithmetic
compound assignments compute at the common width, convert back to the left
width, and evaluate their lvalue once.

Every changed x87 result is stored immediately at its C width. A float rounds
into a fresh four-byte semantic slot. A double receives a fresh private
eight-byte snapshot. The unchanged libm_tanh_impl expression
(e1 - e2) / (e1 + e2) pins nested double arithmetic, and the following float
helper slice pins width conversion. At an ellipsis or unprototyped call, the
frontend promotes float to double after lvalue conversion. Arguments occupy
four-byte or eight-byte cdecl slots, floating results return in x87 ST0, and
va_arg(double) advances by eight bytes. All six comparisons work at a
matching width or widen a mixed pair to double. They produce a signed int and
follow IEEE unordered rules, so only != is true for a NaN operand. A
comparison result can control a statement, but a bare floating value remains
an invalid truth operand.

The decoder-driven model checks width-specific x87 loads and stores, IEEE
rounding cases, mixed expressions, UCOMISS and UCOMISD comparisons, call
alignment, and frame state. The comparison execution proof includes signed
zero, infinities, quiet NaN, and signaling NaN. The arithmetic model does not
execute native x87 code.

The public frontend publishes decimal float and double constants as exact IEEE
binary32 or binary64 bits. It uses bounded integer arithmetic and rounds to
nearest with ties to even, so the self-hosted compiler does not depend on a
host floating library. Static-duration scalar and aggregate leaves use a
second integer-only IEEE evaluator for unary signs, addition, subtraction,
multiplication, division, comparisons, casts, scalar truth, short-circuit
logic, and conditional selection. Represented file and block enumerators and
signed or unsigned integers through 64 bits can feed that evaluator. It rounds
after each operation at the expression width, preserves signed zero, and
writes the final bits to read-only, writable, or zero-filled storage. The IR
and SSE path cover represented runtime integer-to-floating conversions,
floating-to-signed conversions, floating-to-unsigned byte or word
conversions, an explicit non-atomic double to unsigned long long cast, and
mixed represented integer and floating arithmetic. Unsigned four-byte input
uses an exact split across the sign boundary. Unsigned-wide output splits
around 2^32 and derives both words through 2^31-safe truncations. Non-atomic
long double values use twelve-byte objects and x87 80-bit memory loads and
stores. Bounded finite normal decimal tokens with an L suffix round an exact
integer ratio to a 64-bit explicit significand with ties to even. The emitter
writes that significand and the positive token's biased exponent as three
exact snapshot words; unary minus supplies the sign. Automatic values use frame snapshots. Static-duration scalars, fixed
arrays, and complete records may contain long-double leaves. Implicit
initialization zeros the complete object; an explicit leaf accepts an integer
constant expression equal to zero. Each leaf occupies twelve zero-filled BSS
bytes, and atomic leaves fail recursively without following pointers. The
shared path converts among float, double, and long double, applies unary plus
and minus, and supports +, -, *, and /. Direct and
indirect fixed, variadic, and unprototyped arguments occupy twelve cdecl
bytes. Functions return the value in x87 ST0, and direct or indirect callers
store it in a twelve-byte snapshot. va_arg(long double) copies twelve bytes
and leaves the cursor at the following four-byte slot.
All six comparisons accept matching long-double operands or a mixed float or
double input. The emitter loads right then left, executes FUCOMIP ST0, ST1,
and discards the surviving x87 value. Signed zeros compare equal, and only !=
is true for an unordered input. Runtime float, double, and automatic long
double values work with unary !, &&, ||, the controlling operand of ?:, the
conditions of if, while, do, and for, and conversion to _Bool. Both signed zeros are
false; finite nonzero values, subnormals, infinities, and NaNs are true.
Hexadecimal floating literals, binary32 and binary64 subnormal literals,
hexadecimal or subnormal long-double literals, decimal ratios beyond the
bounded parser, nonzero or floating static initializers, integer conversions involving long double other than
_Bool, runtime conversion to unsigned four-byte integers, other
floating-to-wide conversions, runtime mixed wide and floating arithmetic or
conditional arms, increment and decrement, general SIMD value semantics,
floating atomic access, and over-aligned object emission remain unfinished.
The exact production SIMD
assembly forms have their own checked path. Matching or mixed-width floating
conditional arms and the four arithmetic compound assignments keep their
established x87 path.

The exact static aggregate proof covers two 24-byte arrays and two 28-byte
records in 104 BSS bytes. Its 415-byte access function has fingerprint
BF01CC71, eight absolute relocations, and six symbols. The hosted i386 runtime
checks every initial zero leaf and marker, then moves 1.5 through file and
block members.

Plain assignment, all ten compound assignments, and prefix and postfix update
work for non-atomic integer bit fields whose declared storage unit is four
bytes and fits inside the record. The record designator is evaluated once, and
compound operations use the target's integer promotions. Partial fields keep
the other bits in their unit. Assignment, compound assignment, and prefix
update return the stored lane after width truncation and signed extension.
Postfix update returns the extracted old value. Volatile 32-bit updates perform
one read and one store. Partial volatile mutation, atomic fields, and other
storage-unit sizes remain unfinished.

An explicit cast to void evaluates its operand once. The shared IR discards a
represented integer, object pointer, function pointer, supported structure, or
floating scalar value. An eight-byte integer or double lvalue is copied into
its private snapshot before the handle is removed. A float keeps its raw value.
A void operand leaves no value on the abstract stack.

Automatic initializer lists work for fixed arrays and complete structures
whose alignment does not exceed four bytes. The lowerer zero-initializes the
full object once. It then evaluates explicit represented integer, pointer,
supported structure, and narrow character-array string leaves in source
order. Eight-byte integer leaves use the same snapshot copy as other wide
object stores. Scalar and structure values store through the selected array
and member path. A string leaf copies the retained bytes with REP MOVSB, so
unused tail elements stay zero. Nested lists, direct designators, and omitted
subobjects keep their
frontend meaning. The i386 emitter uses REP STOSB for the initial zeroing pass.

The shared path carries compatible, complete structure values through lvalue
conversion, automatic expression initialization, plain and chained
assignment, matching conditional expressions, discarded expressions, fixed
direct and indirect calls, parameters, and returns. This includes casts to
void and other discarded structure expressions. Each lvalue conversion and
structure call result receives its own frame snapshot. Copies cover the full
target object size, so a supported structure may contain wide or floating
members even when an operation on that member remains outside the same-kind
arithmetic boundary.

A structure snapshot may also contain nested union storage because the copy
preserves every target byte. A scalar member can be loaded directly from a
returned structure value. A union used directly as a parameter or result and
an aggregate member selected from a structure rvalue remain unfinished.

A direct four-byte integer literal zero can form a represented function-pointer
null.
An object-pointer null written as ((void *)0), or with a computed integer
constant zero inside that cast, can also convert to its destination pointer
type without changing the four-byte value. The typed conversion keeps a
frontend proof bit. Linear IR rejects a missing or misplaced bit and does not
treat an ordinary runtime void pointer as a null constant.
Object pointers can convert to signed or unsigned eight-byte integers with a
zero high word, and conversion back keeps the low word. Computed or nonzero
function-pointer casts and conversions between function pointers and wide
integers remain unsupported. Static compatible character and void pointers
accept an ordinary string literal through parentheses or a macro. Compiler
head also keeps a static string or linked binding address through an explicit
cast between non-atomic pointer types. A cast through an integer and static
address arithmetic outside the represented addend path remain unfinished.

An external array with an unspecified bound can produce its linked address and
decay to the compatible element pointer when the element type is complete.
Indexing uses the element's target size, and member access keeps the normal
record offset. The array itself remains incomplete and cannot be loaded as a
value.

Pointer qualification accepts the safe char ** to char *const * conversion.
It rejects char ** to const char ** because that would add a qualifier at an
unsafe nested level. It also rejects removing the nested const.

The exact hosted gate covers 33 strict C11 roots and two GNU-enabled runtime
roots under the checked i386 Linux profile. It includes the 19-source tool
union, kernel/lang/as_elf.cc, the runtime implementation and probe, and all
fourteen Toolchain contracts. Thirty-one strict roots use only the Toolchain
and hosted declaration roots. The assembler ELF adapter and its contract use
a separate two-root bridge that can also include /kernel/lang. The GNU profile
is limited to the runtime implementation and probe. The old 64-bit hosted
profiles have no roots. Stage-two and stage-three CupidC compile every
contract, CupidLD links the static executables, and the checked cohort rejects
a byte difference in any of sixteen new objects or fifteen executables. Test
programs stay outside the 19-source fixed-point plan because they do not
contribute to a tool image.

The repository runtime supplies the checked file, heap, memory, string, errno,
getcwd, fixed-width integer, and formatted-output interfaces used by the five
commands. Its public slice includes printf, puts, snprintf, fputc, fputs,
memmove, and strstr. Decimal and hexadecimal formatting handles int, long,
and long long values, including zero-padded widths. String formatting accepts
a fixed or argument-supplied precision. CupidC emits the runtime and its .cc
behavior contract. CupidASM assembles _start and the int 0x80 system-call
boundary. CupidLD links five deterministic static Linux i386 tools plus the
contract without unresolved symbols. The runtime executable checks allocation,
tail release, files, seeks, errors, arguments, formatting, memory comparison,
and strings.

The cupidc driver accepts one C11 input, definitions, undefinitions, forced
inputs, GNU or freestanding mode, ordered include roots, and an ELF32 object
output. -I enables quoted and angle lookup. --include-angle enables angle
lookup only. Repeatable -include options run before the primary source in
caller order. These path options accept native paths or absolute logical paths
under --root. Failed compilation preserves an existing output.

The checked seed reproduces the exact audited Doom-tree profile without
source-shaping workarounds. It keeps the sound driver's empty volatile memory barrier
as a compiler ordering point and emits no instruction for it. Its static
scalar evaluator folds the unchanged fixed-point table in
kernel/doom/src/am_map.cc. The explicit --doom-compat profile represents the
five old-style calls in i_system.cc. It also permits the eleven audited,
bit-preserving conversions between unqualified function pointers and
unqualified four-byte data or void pointers in m_menu.cc, p_saveg.cc,
p_ceilng.cc, and p_plats.cc. Strict C and plain GNU mode still reject those
implicit conversions, and explicit function/data casts remain outside Linear
IR. A one-active-member union initializer emits info.cc. Narrow
unsigned int color fields keep their member identity when they promote to
signed int in ordinary expressions, which compiles i_video.cc.
The checked seed emits all 80 audited Doom-tree objects.

The production wrapper emits the three separate compatibility roots. The
explicit static string cast in doom_libc_stubs.cc and the dglibc jump block keep
their source meaning. Two checked-seed compiles produce the same object for
each root. All 83 sources use .cc and compile through the checked seed.
The wrapper freezes the selected source and all 291 profile headers. Its
input manifest fixes exact three-source and 80-source memberships and rejects
source removal, header drift, symbolic links, and NTFS junctions. The wrapper
also scans visible .c and .cc files beneath the Doom tree before and after
compilation. A legacy .c file or an unlisted .cc file fails the scan. The
g_game.cc object keeps two R_386_32 static subobject relocations with addend
4; direct-call R_386_PC32 relocations still require addend -4.

Earlier private four-CPU boots passed the full frontier with e1000 and
RTL8139. Each NIC ran two explicit missing-IWAD launches in one boot, required
two returns to the shell, and then completed dglibc_test. This image carries
no WAD, so those checks did not prove gameplay, game input or audio, menu
save/load, or persistence across reboot.
The fixed asset-free frontier now runs doom, then
doom -iwad /disk/missing.wad, requires the shell-return marker, and runs a
fresh CupidC-built ls. This pins discovery guidance and explicit missing-file
recovery without treating either launch as gameplay. ADR 0232 records the
gate.

Doom config and save paths use shared errno and checked dglibc streams. Known
relative names resolve beneath /home/doom. Config parsing is bounded and each
shell session restores registered defaults before loading. Config and save
writers close a temporary file, then use native same-mount rename. HomeFS and
RamFS reject busy replacement. A failed block-cache fill cannot relabel its
victim. FAT16 reports missing, handle, I/O, invalid-name, and busy opens
separately, blocks changes to live entries, enforces one-level 8.3 paths, and
flushes a replacement or deletion before it frees the detached chain. HomeFS
rejects malformed containers and duplicate mounts, reserves HOMEFS.SYS against
raw FAT mutation, and lets nested mutation batches publish once at their outer
end. dglibc_test uses test-only files to exercise these paths and requires the
final batch publication. No power-cut injection or WAD-backed save/load proof
exists yet.

The five static i386 Linux tools reach a stage-two to stage-three fixed point
under WSL. Generation-one CupidC, CupidASM, and CupidLD build all 19 C objects,
a fresh startup object, and the five stage-two images. The stage-two producer
trio repeats that work for stage three. Every object and linked CupidC,
CupidASM, CupidDis, CupidLD, and CupidObj image matches byte for byte. Both
stages also agree on all five help paths, twelve successful operations, and
eight failure cases.

The repository carries those exact five images as a checked i386 Linux seed.
Its manifest binds their hashes, sizes, target ABI, source revision, producer
lineage, exact source plan, startup, and five link orders before execution.
The current CupidC image is 2,582,400 bytes. Its SHA-256 is
03084115bcacb1987db5513c8a8be9b7d884029b03ab4b212bf40d997871ae79
and its source revision is c31f062fc67c78b553919c2600dd953d252cb58b.
It retains the complete 83-root Doom frontier, current GNU entity metadata,
x87 and SSE memory forms, descriptor and segment assembly, every unchanged
libm assembly effect, the exact dglibc jump block, pointer-preserving static
address casts, exact naked IPI entries, the kernel-entry BSS clear with a
nonzero page-aligned stack top, and the active packed SSE2 statements. It also
carries runtime floating truth, bounded decimal long double constants,
conversion to _Bool, and the returns-twice direct-call boundary. Its shared
declaration frontend recognizes Cupid's
sized scalar, Boolean, and vector spellings under the Cupid profile while
strict C11 keeps the names available to ordinary programs. CupidASM and
CupidDis in the checked seed carry the 596-row shared x86 catalogue with
forward x87 stack subtraction and canonical SHRD. CupidDis also carries typed
raw code and data ranges. CupidObj carries installation-source generation,
transactional kernel-symbol source generation, and sequential-JPEG validation.
The 279,004-byte image applies
one overflow-safe 512-path request limit, preserves mixed home-asset order,
rejects distinct paths that map to the same wrapped symbol, and renders the
packed symbol source from canonical CupidDis text. The 5,440-byte seed manifest
has SHA-256
1302d48c541850b5248df05d07a8f4d7a68fe070dd6118edadbecd280b309ad1.
ADR 0234 records the current five-tool promotion.
Checked-seed CupidObj validates sequential SOF0 and SOF1 marker streams in its
wrap-jpeg command, then applies the ordinary byte-exact wrapper. The normal
JPEG recipe retains Python validation until its separate ownership transfer.
ADR 0231 records the source capability, and ADR 0234 records seed carriage.
The harness freezes the verified manifest and binaries and copies the exact
bytes of 41 current inputs, including link.ld, into a private compiler root.
The seed producer trio builds stage two below that root, and the stage-two trio
builds stage three there. The private and live closures are checked before the
first stage, after each stage, and after the behavior checks. All 19 C objects,
startup, and five linked images match byte for byte. Five help checks, twelve
successful operations, and eight failure cases pass at both stages.
The 774.524-second post-promotion rebuild reproduced all five seed images at
stage two and repeated the complete fixed point. ADR 0234 records the
transition, promotion, and reproof.

make verify-bootstrap-seed checks the seed without executing it.
make bootstrap-from-seed publishes both stages, behavior evidence, and a report
under build/bootstrap/checked-seed only after the full gate passes. The output
must start absent or empty. Linux runs the static tools directly. Windows uses
WSL. Native contract runners and hosted development commands still use a host
compiler. Normal OS objects do not.

Structure arguments occupy inline cdecl stack storage in parameter order, and
each argument is rounded up to four bytes. The caller clears ABI padding before
copying arguments. A structure-returning call passes a hidden destination
pointer before the explicit arguments. The callee reads that pointer at EBP+8,
reads its first explicit argument at EBP+12, copies the result into the
destination, returns the pointer in EAX, and uses RET 4. The caller cleans the
explicit argument bytes.

Supported direct and indirect calls place ESP on a sixteen-byte boundary
immediately before CALL. The emitter chooses zero, four, eight, or twelve bytes
of padding from the function frame, live Linear IR stack, and outgoing
target-sized argument area. Prototyped, variadic, unprototyped, nested,
structure, and wide calls follow the same rule.

For a variadic call, the frontend applies lvalue conversion, array and function
decay, integer promotion, and float-to-double promotion to the ellipsis
arguments as required. Every
call instruction owns a contiguous slice of post-conversion
actual argument types in a packed Linear IR array. Named slots use declared
parameter types after compatibility checking. Unnamed slots use the packed
actual types. A shared validator rejects gaps, overlaps, invalid types,
trailing entries, and metadata on non-call instructions. The emitter uses the
validated slice and actual count for cdecl order, slot widths, the saved
indirect callee, stack alignment, and caller cleanup. Direct
and indirect calls can pass represented four-byte integers and pointers,
signed and unsigned eight-byte integers, existing doubles, or source floats
promoted to double through an ellipsis. Existing long doubles also cross in
twelve-byte slots. An eight-byte unnamed value uses two adjacent words; a
long double uses three. Arguments occupy increasing addresses in source
order, with lower words first. Each argument still has one abstract IR handle.
An indirect callee remains below the argument handles while the emitter
prepares the outgoing area.

In GNU C mode, __builtin_va_list is a target char pointer cursor. The shared
frontend retains start, argument, copy, and end operations. The i386 emitter
starts the cursor after the full width of the final named cdecl parameter. A
four-byte pointer, integer, or enum read advances the stored cursor by four
bytes. A signed or unsigned eight-byte integer, 64-bit enum, or double is
copied into a fresh private snapshot and advances the cursor by eight bytes.
A long-double read copies twelve bytes and advances the cursor by twelve.
Every represented width keeps the i386 cursor on four-byte slot alignment.
Execution contracts read successive wide integer, double, and long-double
slots through the original cursor and the first slot through a copied cursor.
The static i386 runtime also reads a four-byte value immediately after a long
double. Nested callers check aligned calls, cleanup, and complete returned
values.
Atomic, float, and aggregate reads remain unsupported. Calling va_arg with
float is invalid C because a variadic float arrives as double. The unchanged
Doom compatibility header parses under its generated profile.

An empty identifier-list definition has zero parameters and keeps its
non-prototype function type. Direct and indirect calls without a prototype
apply default argument promotions to every argument. Each call keeps its actual
count and post-conversion type slice in Linear IR. The i386 emitter accepts
represented four-byte integers and pointers, signed or unsigned eight-byte
integers, existing doubles or long doubles, and source floats promoted to
double. The static i386 runtime executes direct and indirect unprototyped
long-double calls.

Block-scope struct and union tags follow lexical C scope. Forward declarations,
same-scope completion, ordinary references, nested shadowing, and restoration
after scope exit are supported. A record tag declared in a function definition's
parameter list remains visible through the outer body and expires with the
definition. A tag-only declaration may use typedef, extern, static, auto,
register, or a represented type qualifier when it introduces a tag, and has no
runtime work. An empty declaration with storage or type qualification cannot
merely repeat a visible tag. A for initializer may use a visible record type or
an anonymous record definition
for its object, but it cannot introduce a named tag or omit the object. An
anonymous record definition can provide the type for an automatic or
block-static object, including Doom's packs array. A block-scope extern object
keeps a lexical alias to one canonical linked object. Compatible repeats share
identity, an incomplete array can be completed, and a visible file-scope
static object keeps internal linkage. The declaration creates no automatic
storage or runtime IR. The exact Doom profile parses all of
kernel/doom/src/d_main.cc, including its local forwardmove and sidemove
declarations. Block typedefs follow the ordinary identifier scope. They retain
stable scalar, record, or function types, allow exact same-type repetition and
nested shadowing, and create no runtime IR, frame storage, symbol, or
relocation.

A block function declaration keeps its lexical name and visible type alongside
one canonical linked function. Plain and extern declarations share compatible
identity. A visible prior declaration contributes to the later alias's
composite type. An expired sibling prototype does not change the type seen by
a later old-style declaration. A visible file-scope static function keeps
internal linkage. A function introduced only inside a block stays out of file
lookup until a later file declaration publishes it. The declaration creates no
runtime IR or storage. Direct calls use R_386_PC32, and function addresses use
R_386_32. Active-source checks cover 27 declarations in nine files.

Block enums keep their enumerators in the ordinary lexical binding stream. A
later enumerator can use an earlier value, nested tags and constants shadow
their outer names, and scope exit restores those names. Definitions work in
declarations, record members, function-definition parameter lists, and block
type names. Function prefixes and expression or initializer activation records
keep the exact point where each name becomes visible. Linear IR checks that
source order before lowering runtime control flow, including case values, loop
headers, variadic reads, aggregate designators, and compound literals.
Represented uses become integer constants with no frame slot, symbol,
relocation, or runtime declaration instruction. File and block enumerators can
also feed static floating arithmetic, comparison, and conditional
expressions. This covers the cursor
constants in the production CupidC-built kernel/gui/desktop.cc object. The
REPL limits in kernel/lang/shell.cc remain a separate active guard. Block
declaration attributes, nested function definitions, nonempty identifier
lists, atomic variadic access, aggregate arguments without declared parameter
types, and aggregate variadic reads remain open.

Block-static objects use static storage in the shared ELF32 path. The
emitter places top-level const objects in .rodata, writable zero-filled objects
in .bss, and other writable objects in .data. Each object receives a local
symbol derived from its absolute block-binding index, so shadowed names remain
distinct. LOCAL_ADDRESS reaches that symbol through an R_386_32 relocation
instead of an EBP-relative frame slot, and the declaration emits no runtime
initialization code. Unused and unreachable block statics still receive
storage.

Block-scope compound literals use one persistent unnamed automatic object per
source site. Their initializer runs each time execution reaches the expression.
The resulting lvalue can pass through ordinary member access, indexing,
address-taking, loads, stores, and calls. Repeated evaluation in one function
invocation reuses the same object. Recursive calls receive a fresh object in
their own frame. Aggregate lists are built in separate staging storage and
committed after every initializer read has finished. A narrow string root
zeros and copies directly into its persistent character array.

One-active-member union initializer lists use the same aggregate paths. A
positional clause selects the first eligible named member. A direct member
designator selects that member instead. Runtime lowering zeros the complete
union before storing the selected value, and static emission writes that
member over zero-filled storage. Multiple clauses and override semantics
remain unfinished.

Runtime narrow string expressions receive local .rodata symbols and R_386_32
relocations. They can decay into pointers for initialization, arguments,
indexing, and returns. Supported structure graphs have alignment no greater
than four bytes and contain no stored volatile or atomic subobjects. Graphs
may contain a nested union. Top-level union and class values remain
unsupported. Static-duration and
variable-length compound literals, the named-aggregate backward-jump alias
case, explicit bit-field initializer leaves, Boolean mutation, direct floating
truth, unsupported conversion widths, floating update, atomic
variadic access, aggregate arguments without declared parameter types,
aggregate variadic reads, wide strings, and literal pooling remain unfinished
in the shared path. A block-static initializer may now take the address of
another block-static object. Static initializers may also reuse the direct
integer initializer of an earlier non-atomic const integer. This is a narrow
Cupid C extension, not an ISO C integer constant expression. It preserves the
unchanged Toolchain object contract's address tables. Mutable, automatic,
atomic, indirect, and non-integer cases remain rejected.

The shared path builds 238 checked-in normal kernel, driver, Doom, and
toolchain objects. It also owns the generated kernel symbol source as the
239th normal CupidC transform. All 239 normal sources use .cc. The checked seed also
compiles the three generated installation tables and the three example ELF
programs. Checked CupidLD links those example programs. The normal Toolchain
cohort is Cupid-built. A host compiler is used only for explicit native
oracles and development commands, while the private in-kernel CupidC compiler
handles embedded runtime compilation.

The checked seed gives weak, section, and unused attributes their declaration
and ELF meaning. It also preserves typed static null pointers, evaluates comma
expressions from left to right, recognizes known-true loops as
non-fallthrough, and supports represented function-pointer casts that keep all
32 target bits. Exact output-only GNU assembly can snapshot general registers,
ESP, EBP, the caller return slot, or EFLAGS. These additions are part of the
normal production path after the frontier, image, and runtime proofs passed.

The checked seed accepts used and __used__ on file-scope objects and
functions. Compatible redeclarations merge the flag into one canonical
entity. Linear IR and object emission validate the frozen metadata. The
attribute does not change current object bytes because every represented
definition is already emitted. The normal build uses this path for
kernel/cpu/ksyms_data.cc.

The checked seed retains noinline and target("general-regs-only") on
canonical file-scope functions. noinline records the request for a future
inliner and does not change current bytes. Each IR function carries the
canonical code generation mask, and emission rejects a mismatch. The target
option rejects compiler-generated floating work while explicit source
assembly stays under its own typed contract. The checked seed also accepts the
exact volatile ldmxcsr %0 form with one addressable, non-atomic 32-bit
integer m input. Linear IR evaluates the address once, and the shared x86
model emits 0F AE 10 at [EAX]. It accepts the exact MOVSS
float-memory round trip in fpu_boot_smoke and its one-way load and store
forms. Each form requires the xmm0 clobber. The shared model emits
F3 0F 10 00 for the load and F3 0F 11 00 for the store through EAX.
It also accepts the exact volatile x87 sine block in stress_sin.
It keeps one modifiable double =m output and one addressable double m input,
permits no clobbers, and evaluates both addresses once in output-then-input
order. The shared model emits FLD, FSIN, and FSTP through EAX with balanced
x87 depth and no frame temporary. The normal build compiles
kernel/cpu/fpu.cc through the checked wrapper. Two checked compiles produce
the same validated 6,620-byte object with SHA-256
14c3ea232b7d4455ceabd561c69293cc5849abae24d9f210aa69d64ed8c8a5cb.
The production object policy decodes fpu_init_cpu and rejects helper calls or
floating work before the CR4 write. It requires FNINIT before one 32-bit
memory LDMXCSR. The four-vCPU gate requires [fpu] SSE2 enabled,
[fpu] boot smoke ok, and FPU boot smoke passed.

The checked seed accepts the exact volatile flags restore used twice by
simd_cpu_has_cpuid. It takes one non-atomic 32-bit integer r input, has no
outputs, and requires one cc clobber. Linear IR keeps the effect as public
metadata. The shared x86 path consumes the value through EAX, pushes it back,
and emits POPF with balanced ESP.

It also accepts the unchanged CPUID input that shares EAX with
its compatible write-only output. The input keeps its a constraint and
names the =a output as its match. Linear IR and emission require both sides
to be represented integers of equal width before loading EAX immediately
before CPUID. Frozen pointer, floating, and aggregate substitutes fail.
Read/write outputs cannot receive a second fixed input. The checked seed emits
all six packed SSE2 statement shapes in kernel/cpu/simd.cc. It checks their
ordered pointer and 32-bit integer inputs plus exact memory and XMM0 through
XMM7 clobbers. The production wrapper freezes the source and its seven-header
closure. Two checked-seed builds produce the same validated 8,768-byte object
with SHA-256
fd280c321b8eb38a90d4f0982d70b8df0364585e3da322eb2c9de722e071f8d4.
The normal recipe now uses this checked object.

The checked seed accepts the exact volatile x87 round-down block in
str_floor. It keeps one modifiable, non-atomic double =m output, one
addressable, non-atomic double m input, and the exact ax plus memory clobber
set. Linear IR evaluates the output address before the input address. After
FLD, the emitter reuses the consumed input-address slot below ESP for the
saved and temporary x87 control words. The pending output address remains
intact. The sequence selects round toward negative infinity for FRNDINT,
restores the incoming control word, and stores the result with balanced x87
depth. A bounded shared-decoder oracle checks eight binary64 inputs under all
four incoming rounding modes without executing native x87 code.

Two exact compiles of the unchanged str_floor helper produce the same
420-byte object. The checked seed also emits the later explicit
double-to-uint64_t casts. The decoder-driven oracle covers zero, positive and
negative fractions, both sides of 2^32, 2^53 minus one, 2^63, the active
1.8e19 guard, and the largest binary64 value below 2^64. Full unchanged
kernel/core/string.cc
compiles twice to the same 14,460-byte object with SHA-256
d48bb6ea18b7124fbefeaca0d5d5ee8a517db950f21ea88e30ededd6c5c2a577.
The production wrapper freezes the source and its two headers, validates the
ELF32 object, and publishes it without a host compiler.

Compiler head accepts the exact operand-free BSS-clear statement only as the
direct first child of the external, prototyped void _start(void) body in
.text.start. It requires the eax, ecx, edi, and memory clobbers, visible
external object declarations for _bss_start and _kernel_end, and no
compiler-managed frame. Frontend statement depth and Linear IR body identity
reject leading, label-wrapped, or otherwise nested copies.

The checked seed loads both symbols through
R_386_32 relocations, derives the doubleword count, clears eax, and emits CLD
plus REP STOSD through the shared x86 model. It accepts a nonzero
stack top written as one through eight hexadecimal digits when it is aligned
to 4 KiB. It places that value in MOV ESP, imm32 while keeping the rest of the
statement exact. The kmain call uses the reset stack residue. If it returns,
_start disables interrupts and remains in a halt loop. The active source
installs 0x01100000 at the top of the fixed two-MiB stack.

The exact fixture has a 27-byte assembly body in a 42-byte function. Its
three relocations name _bss_start, _kernel_end, and kmain. Two runs of the
Cupid-built compiler emit kernel/core/kernel.cc as the same 25,920-byte object
with SHA-256
ed42676ad0d7f16b1fb83442ead1b0082781324dca719104922099cee34b5ab0.
The normal image built with this object passes the four-CPU frontier contract
on both supported NICs. The production wrapper freezes the source and its
63-header closure, and the normal recipe uses this checked object.

The checked seed accepts the four exact descriptor-table and
segment-register statements in kernel/smp/percpu.cc. A packed
six-byte object supplies LGDT through m. The two data-segment templates
require the exact ax plus memory clobbers, the standalone CS reload keeps its
memory clobber, and the GS form takes one represented 16-bit selector
through r.

Linear IR carries the GDTR address or selector value. The shared x86 emitter
writes LGDT, DS, ES, SS, and GS. It reloads CS through a relative CALL, JMP,
and RETF trampoline, so the object needs no compiler-local label relocation.
Two complete compiles produce the same 6,760-byte object with SHA-256
3c2c6f0e00e5edec1ca16cba91e9fc593d1c42e24f4ebd3591e5f574fb0dd772.
The checked normal wrapper owns the object.

The checked seed retains naked and __naked__ on a canonical file-scope
void (void) function. The represented body is one complete IPI entry
statement. The reschedule and call forms emit PUSHAL, a direct call to a
declared C function, POPAL, and IRET. The panic form emits CLI, HLT, and a
backward jump. The emitter adds no C prologue, local frame, epilogue, or
synthetic return. The earlier smp.c compiler proof produced an 8,444-byte
object with SHA-256
806509a6dd1ac7eb34b7ffcb67a1f8852950663a274145584d0260da76dcba54.
The checked production root is kernel/smp/smp.cc. Its object remains 8,444
bytes and has SHA-256
bd3189b2a1a6d15728c559172f5d6acca0889103428085cec8cc1024742a22d1.
The existing __FILE__ diagnostic accounts for the new hash.

The checked seed also accepts the exact volatile
call 1f\n1: popl %0 form in the stack-trace helpers from kernel/lang/as.cc
and kernel/lang/cupidc.cc. It takes one modifiable four-byte integer =r
output. Cupid's x86 model emits a zero-displacement CALL followed by POP r32,
which captures the address of the pop and restores ESP without a relocation.
Both normal recipes compile reproducibly as validated i386 ELF32 objects
through checked CupidC wrappers. Other call templates and general
inline-assembly labels remain unsupported.
>endtree

>h2 C Compatibility Notes
>tree open C Compatibility Notes
CupidC remains a small in-OS compiler, not hosted GCC C.

Accepted for source compatibility:
  long, short, signed, unsigned, long long
  extern, inline, register, restrict
  private compatibility attributes; shared weak, section, unused, used,
  noinline, general-regs-only target, and exact naked IPI semantics at
  the checked seed
  labels and goto
  constant integer expressions in enum/array bounds

Generated code still targets CupidOS's 32-bit flat kernel ABI. Use explicit
bindings for data that truly needs wider or protocol-specific handling.
>button Run Wide Types Test | shell:feature24_widetypes
>endtree

>h2 Floating Point
>tree open Floating Point
CupidC supports hardware floating point and SSE/SSE2 SIMD.

Scalar types:
- float   (4 bytes, SSE scalar)
- double  (8 bytes, SSE scalar)

SIMD types:
- float4  (16 bytes, 4 packed floats)
- double2 (16 bytes, 2 packed doubles)

Operators +, -, *, / work on scalar float/double with implicit
promotion (int -> float -> double). SIMD types need matching types
for +/-/*//; mixing scalar and SIMD is a compile error.

The six scalar comparison operators return int. Matching float or double
widths compare directly. A mixed pair compares as double. Signed zero equals
positive zero, and only != is true when either operand is NaN.

Scalar float and double values work directly with !, if, ?:, while, for, and
do-while. Positive and negative zero are false. Every nonzero value,
infinity, and NaN is true. Void expressions, structures by value, and SIMD
vectors are not valid truth operands.

One-dimensional fixed float and double array symbols keep their declared
element type. Global, automatic, block-static, and persistent REPL arrays
reserve four or eight bytes per element and use indirect SSE loads and stores.
Plain indexed assignment uses the scalar conversion rules. Indexed +=, -=,
*=, and /= stay at the element width. One-, two-, and three-dimensional fixed
float and double arrays keep the remaining row stride through each subscript.
sizeof(array[index]) reports that row without running the index. Depth-one
floating pointers retain their width through address expressions, returns,
function and method array parameters, dereference, subscripts, scaled direct
pointer updates, and assignment. Structure and class objects, their arrays,
and their pointers keep scalar floating fields and one-dimensional fixed
floating field arrays. Bounds and dimension products are checked before
storage is reserved. Deeper floating pointers, indirect floating updates,
pointer-to-array types, and assignment through a
pointer-valued floating field subscript remain unsupported.

Matching float4 and double2 values support direct +, -, *, and /. Fixed
one-dimensional arrays work in global, automatic, block-static, and persistent
REPL storage with 16-byte elements and unaligned-safe packed loads and stores.
Indexed assignment and +=, -=, *=, and /= retain the vector type and allow a
following lane read. Every direct operator keeps the written left value in the
machine destination. MIN and MAX intrinsics keep the second input for NaN and
equal signed-zero cases. A both-NaN ADD or MUL may carry either input payload,
depending on the processor or emulator. SIMD pointers, multidimensional arrays,
record fields, new, array parameters, and call ABI transport remain unsupported.

Direct functions and methods with parsed fixed parameter types convert
represented integer, char, float, and double arguments to the declared cdecl
slot type. Represented pointer categories and integer null forms can fill a
pointer slot. A represented object pointer can fill a fixed int or unsigned
int slot as one unchanged i386 word. Narrow and floating destinations remain
rejected. A parsed variadic tail widens float to double and promotes char
to int. Function-pointer calls, kernel bindings, and calls without fixed
parameter metadata keep their source-width slots. Character operands undergo
integer promotion and use the scalar integer conversion path when mixed with
floating values or explicitly cast. Fresh subscript metadata prevents a
pointer result from inheriting an earlier array stride.

The kernel binding table preserves each function's declared result. Its 325
value-returning entries include 208 promoted integers, 40 unsigned words, 25
float, 25 double, 19 character pointers, and eight other pointers. The other
231 entries return void. Conditions therefore use the value that a binding
actually returns instead of stale expression state.

Prefix and postfix ++ and -- work on scalar float and double variables.
Each update adds or subtracts an exact 1.0 at the variable's width. A postfix
expression returns the old floating payload after storing the new value.
Locals, parameters, globals, statement updates, and for-loop increments use
the same typed path. Arrays, structures, function pointers, and SIMD vectors
are not valid update targets.

Runtime unary plus preserves char, int, float, and double operands. Runtime
unary minus uses integer NEG for char and int, while float and double change
only their IEEE-754 sign bit. The floating path preserves negative zero,
infinities, and payload bits. A non-arithmetic operand receives a clear type
error, and a later REPL expression can still compile.

Casts use CVTSI2SS/CVTTSS2SI/CVTSS2SD form:
  (int)3.7      -> 3 (truncating)
  (float)5      -> 5.0
  (double)1.5f  -> widens

Element access on SIMD:
  float4 v;  v.x, v.y, v.z, v.w
  double2 w; w.x, w.y

Intrinsics: _mm_*_ps (17) and _mm_*_pd (11) are recognized by
name and inlined as SSE opcodes. See kernel/cpu/simd_intrin.h.

libm is available via the BIND_T table (25 operations, 50 symbols
with f-variants): sqrt, sin, cos, tan, atan, atan2, exp, exp2, log,
log2, pow, asin, acos, sinh, cosh, tanh, cbrt, hypot, nextafter,
fabs, floor, ceil, round, trunc, fmod.

printf supports %f, %e, %g, and precision forms like %.3f.

Decimal float and double tokens are converted as exact ratios and rounded once
to the requested IEEE width, with ties going to even. An f suffix selects
binary32 before rounding. Numeric tokens may contain up to 95 characters,
including the suffix. Hexadecimal floating and long double literals remain
unsupported.

Decimal and hexadecimal integer tokens cover the represented uint32_t range.
Larger values and empty hexadecimal bodies receive focused errors. An optional
u or U counts toward the 95-character token limit and remains part of one
rejected overlong token. An explicit INT_MAX enum value is valid, while an
implicit successor reports enum value overflow.

Fixed arrays require positive counts and checked byte products. Record padding,
field growth, final alignment, global or REPL capacity, and cumulative local
frames stay within signed compiler limits. REPL rollback restores the complete
record table, not only its count.

Adjacent ordinary string tokens form one null-terminated data object. Each
token may contain at most 1,023 decoded bytes, while the joined value can use
the remaining private data section. Automatic expressions, file-scope pointer
initializers, and persistent REPL declarations share this behavior. An
overlong token or data-section exhaustion produces an error instead of a
truncated string.

See also: bin/feature12_float.cc through bin/feature16_asm_fpu.cc.
feature13_double checks decimal payloads, typed floating matrices, pointers,
record fields, row-sized unevaluated sizeof, scalar calls, and libm.
feature14_simd checks packed operators, fixed arrays, NaN handling, and SSE
intrinsics. The
Browser's binary64 token and AST tables use the fixed double-array path.
See also: 14FPU-SSE.CTXT for full FPU/SSE reference.
[Open FPU/SSE guide](open:14FPU-SSE.CTXT)
>endtree

>h2 Preprocessor
>tree open Preprocessor
CupidC supports:
- #pragma once

Use this at the top of reusable include-style .cc files (for example
bin/ctxt.cc) to prevent duplicate-definition errors when a file is
included multiple times.
>endtree

>h2 Useful Bindings (Partial List)
>tree open Useful Bindings (Partial List)
Console:
  print, println, putchar, print_int, print_hex, clear_screen

Memory:
  kmalloc, kfree, memstats, heap_check_integrity, detect_memory_leaks

VFS:
  vfs_open, vfs_read, vfs_write, vfs_seek, vfs_close
  vfs_stat, vfs_readdir, vfs_mkdir, vfs_unlink, vfs_rename
  vfs_read_all, vfs_write_all, vfs_read_text, vfs_write_text, vfs_copy_file
  Rename is native within one mount and returns EXDEV across mounts. The
  write-all and copy helpers include destination close in their result.

Clipboard:
  clipboard_set, clipboard_get, clipboard_len

Shell/process:
  get_cwd, set_cwd, resolve_path, get_history_count, get_history_entry
  process_list, process_kill, spawn_test, yield, exit, exec
  get_args

Time/system:
  uptime_ms, get_cpu_mhz, timer_get_frequency
  rtc_hour/minute/second/day/month/year/weekday, rtc_epoch
  date_full_string, time_string, etc.

Debug:
  dump_stack_trace, dump_registers, peek_byte, print_hex_byte
  set_log_level, get_log_level_name, print_log_buffer

Disk/cache:
  blockcache_sync, blockcache_stats
  blockcache_sync returns the device writeback result.

Swap (opt-in):
  swap_init, swap_kmalloc, swap_pin, swap_unpin, swap_free

GUI/graphics:
  Large gfx2d and desktop UI binding set is available in GUI mode.
  See the windowed graphics sections below.

Notepad integration:
  notepad_get_open_path(out_path, out_save_path)

>button Show Uptime | shell:time
>button List /bin | shell:ls /bin
>endtree

>h2 Graphics/Desktop Bindings
>tree open Graphics/Desktop Bindings
Available for GUI app and desktop-oriented workflows:

The complete 46-name gfxgui cohort exposes effects, bitmap-font assets,
affine transforms, GUI module initialization, and themes. All 104 runnable
top-level programs now pass private AOT compilation. The fixed runtime gate
first emits /gfxgui_test, then runs /bin/gfxgui_test.cc through private JIT.
It requires setup, frame 0, frame 240, cleanup, and JIT completion. An
unresolved native symbol fails the gate immediately. The test keeps its
temporary theme, BMP, font, and ELF files in RamFS. Its transform path uses
16.16 operands for the 3/2 scale and translation. It keeps the full 32.32
determinant and inverse translation arithmetic in checked 64-bit form. The
success path requires an exact generated-font pixel, an isolated filtered
surface pixel with unchanged screen state, and center and off-center
transformed-image pixels. It also checks an off-origin point after a 90-degree
rotation and nonuniform scale, then proves that pop restores identity.

Effects and assets:
- gfx2d_blur_box, gfx2d_blur_gaussian, gfx2d_blur_motion
- gfx2d_brightness, gfx2d_contrast, gfx2d_saturation, gfx2d_hue_shift
- gfx2d_edges, gfx2d_emboss, gfx2d_posterize, gfx2d_noise
- gfx2d_convolve_3x3, gfx2d_convolve_5x5
- gfx2d_font_load, gfx2d_font_set_default, gfx2d_text_ex, gfx2d_font_free

Transforms and themes:
- gfx2d_push_transform, gfx2d_pop_transform, gfx2d_reset_transform
- gfx2d_translate, gfx2d_rotate, gfx2d_scale
- gfx2d_set_matrix, gfx2d_get_matrix, gfx2d_transform_point
- ui_theme_set, ui_theme_reset_default, ui_theme_load, ui_theme_save
- ui_theme_windows95, ui_theme_dark_mode, ui_theme_pastel_dream

- gfx2d_text_wrap(x, y, w, str, color, font)
- gfx2d_surface_blit_scaled(handle, x, y, w, h)
- gfx2d_icon_draw_named(label, x, y, color)
- gfx2d_glow_circle(cx, cy, radius, color, alpha)
- gfx2d_light_sweep(x, y, w, h, tick, color, alpha)
- gfx2d_popup_menu(x, y, items, count)
- gfx2d_tooltip(x, y, text)
- gfx2d_message_dialog(message)

Desktop app source metadata:
- //app: Simple Name
- //app: label="Name"; desc="Description"; icon=app; x=120; y=80;

During the /bin .cc scan, this metadata creates launchable desktop icons.
>endtree

>h2 Windowed App Bindings
>tree open Windowed App Bindings
Window lifecycle:
- gui_win_create(title, x, y, w, h) -> int window_id (or -1)
- gui_win_is_open(window_id) -> int
- gui_win_close(window_id)

Window content geometry:
- gui_win_content_x(window_id) -> int
- gui_win_content_y(window_id) -> int
- gui_win_content_w(window_id) -> int
- gui_win_content_h(window_id) -> int

Window input + present:
- gui_win_poll_key(window_id) -> packed key or -1
  packed format: (scancode << 8) | char
- gui_win_flip(window_id)  (compatibility wrapper)

Retained-surface paint/present flow (preferred):
- gui_win_begin_paint(window_id) -> int
  binds the window's retained content surface as the active gfx2d target
- gui_win_end_paint(window_id) -> int
  unbinds the active surface and marks the window content dirty
- gui_win_present(window_id) -> int
  asks the compositor to present dirty hosted windows

Explicit invalidation helpers:
- gui_win_invalidate(window_id) -> int
- gui_win_invalidate_rect(window_id, x, y, w, h) -> int

Terminal/shell buffer surface:
- shell_buf_rows() / shell_buf_cols()
- shell_buf_char(row, col)
- shell_buf_color(row, col)
- shell_cursor_x() / shell_cursor_y()
- shell_send_key(scancode, ch)

Input helper:
- keyboard_ctrl_held() -> int

Desktop background control:
- desktop_bg_set_mode_anim(theme)
- desktop_bg_set_mode_solid(color)
- desktop_bg_set_mode_gradient(top, bottom)
- desktop_bg_set_mode_tiled_pattern(pattern, fg, bg)
- desktop_bg_set_mode_tiled_bmp(path)
- desktop_bg_set_mode_bmp(path)
- desktop_bg_get_mode()
- desktop_bg_get_solid_color()
- desktop_bg_get_anim_theme()
- desktop_bg_get_tiled_pattern()
- desktop_bg_get_tiled_use_bmp()
>endtree

>h2 Fast Workflow
>tree open Fast Workflow
1) Create source:
   ed /home/bin/mycmd.cc

2) Run directly:
   mycmd

3) If needed, run with explicit compiler:
   cupidc /home/bin/mycmd.cc

4) Produce ELF artifact:
   ccc /home/bin/mycmd.cc -o /home/mycmd

5) Inspect generated instructions:
   cc -d /home/bin/mycmd.cc
   dis /home/mycmd

>button Edit A File | shell:ed /home/bin/hello.cc
>endtree

>h2 Error Handling Tips
>tree open Error Handling Tips
- CupidC fails fast and reports line-oriented compiler errors.
- If behavior is unclear, reduce the program to a small reproduction, then
  restore one feature at a time.
- Keep stack usage modest.
- Prefer kmalloc for large buffers.
>endtree

>h2 When To Use CupidC Vs ELF C
>tree open When To Use CupidC Vs ELF C
Use CupidC when:
- You want fastest edit/run loop inside CupidOS shell.
- You want direct access to many kernel helper bindings.

Use ELF C (see 05ELFPROG.CTXT) when:
- You want a checked external CupidC and CupidLD build.
- You want a stable syscall-table ABI style entrypoint.
>endtree

